  <h1>India’s Role in BRICS+ Expansion</h1>
  <div><em>Newsweek | Date: 2024–25 • ~1200 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The BRICS grouping — Brazil, Russia, India, China, and South Africa — was initially conceived as an economic coalition of emerging powers. By 2024–25, it had transformed into BRICS+, with six new members including Saudi Arabia, UAE, Egypt, Ethiopia, Argentina, and Iran. This expansion reflects the shifting global order and dissatisfaction with Western-dominated institutions. Newsweek analyzed India’s pivotal role in this transition: balancing opportunities for multipolarity with challenges of managing contradictions within the bloc.
________________________________________
Why BRICS Expanded
1.	Dissatisfaction with Western Institutions
o	IMF, World Bank, and UNSC reforms remain stalled.
o	Developing nations sought alternative platforms.
2.	Energy & Trade Interests
o	Inclusion of Gulf countries brings oil power into BRICS+.
o	Argentina and Ethiopia add agricultural and African representation.
3.	Geopolitical Signaling
o	BRICS expansion challenges G7’s dominance.
o	Positions the bloc as voice of Global South.
________________________________________
India’s Role in the Expansion
1.	Balancing China and Russia
o	India ensured expansion didn’t become China-dominated.
o	Supported inclusion of diverse countries (e.g., Gulf monarchies, African states).
2.	Champion of Global South
o	As G20 president (2023), India highlighted Global South concerns.
o	Advocated for development finance, climate justice, and debt relief.
3.	Pragmatism in Diplomacy
o	India maintained relations with West while strengthening BRICS.
o	Avoided taking extreme anti-West positions, unlike Russia and Iran.
________________________________________
Opportunities for India
•	Economic Gains:
o	Energy security through ties with Saudi Arabia, UAE, and Iran.
o	Expanded trade with Africa and Latin America.
•	Strategic Leverage:
o	Greater voice in multipolar world.
o	Counterweight to Western dominance in IMF/World Bank.
•	Cultural & Political Influence:
o	India projects itself as the bridge between developed and developing worlds.
________________________________________
Challenges for India
1.	China Factor:
o	China seeks to dominate BRICS, using economic clout and Belt and Road.
o	India wary of China–Pakistan axis leveraging BRICS platforms.
2.	Contradictory Interests:
o	Members like Saudi Arabia vs Iran, or India vs China, dilute cohesion.
o	BRICS+ risks becoming symbolic rather than effective.
3.	Western Pushback:
o	Closer BRICS+ ties with Russia and Iran may complicate India’s partnerships with US/EU.
4.	Institutional Effectiveness:
o	BRICS Bank (NDB) underutilized.
o	Limited ability to set global norms compared to IMF/WTO.
________________________________________
Geopolitical Implications
•	For US and Europe: BRICS+ seen as alternative pole to G7.
•	For Global South: India’s leadership strengthens its claim as representative voice.
•	For Multipolarity: Reinforces trend toward fragmented, multi-center world order.
________________________________________
UPSC & PSIR Relevance
•	GS-II (IR): India’s role in multilateralism, South–South cooperation.
•	GS-III (Economy): Trade, finance, energy security.
•	Essay:
o	“Is BRICS+ the future of multipolarity?”
o	“Global South and the search for equitable governance.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Theories of multipolarity, regionalism, and South–South cooperation.
________________________________________</p>
